  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nA north to northeast wind shift will begin moving into the area on \nMonday. This boundary does not appear like it will have much impact \non temperatures, but models are showing an increase of moisture \nbehind the boundary. Winds are expected to increase out of the \nnortheast behind the boundary which may help to mix out some of the \nlow-level moisture. There are uncertainties as to how much of an \ninfluence this boundary will have on temperatures, moisture, and \ncloud cover for Monday. There is higher confidence across north \ncentral Kansas for the combination of temperatures (around 100 to \n106 degrees) and moisture to produce heat index values near or above \n110 degrees for Monday. For this reason, the Heat Advisory across \nthis area was upgraded to an Extreme Heat Warning which is in effect \nuntil 10 PM Monday. There is lower confidence (due to the above \nmentioned factors) across south central and central Nebraska so \nthose areas were not upgraded at this time. Although, the Nebraska \ncounties bordering Kansas have the highest likelihood of \nreaching Extreme Heat criteria on Monday and there is fairly \nhigh confidence (around 70%) that some areas in these counties \nmay have heat index values near/around 110 degrees on Monday. \nHeat index values across our Nebraska counties are expected to \nrange from around 101 in Ord to around 106 to 110 degrees across\nthe far southern row of counties. Showers and thunderstorms \nwill be possible (up to around a 50% chance) Monday evening and \nnight as an upper disturbance moves overhead from the west. The \nhighest chances will be across western portions of the area \n(mainly west of the Tri-Cities area). Some of these storms may \nbecome strong to marginally severe with wind gusts up to around \n60 MPH being the main threat. The showers and storms will move \neastward as the night continues. \n\nCooler temperatures are expected beginning Tuesday (highs in the \nupper 80s to low/mid 90s) as a surface high moves across the area. \nHigh temperatures in the 80s and 90s will continue through the end \nof the week. Low temperatures will be in the 60s and 70s. Various \nrain and storm chances will continue Tuesday through Thursday night \nas upper disturbances move over the area.",
